1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,312
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is AP Computer Science

2
00:00:05,312 --> 00:00:11,271
Principles, Big Idea 3, part four. We'll
use libraries and randomness, and discover

3
00:00:11,271 --> 00:00:13,568
what computers can and can't do.

4
00:00:14,741 --> 00:00:16,960
Programmers rarely start from nothing.

5
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:20,994
They build on code that other people
already wrote and tested.

6
00:00:22,144 --> 00:00:26,884
A software library is a collection of
procedures you can use in your own

7
00:00:26,884 --> 00:00:27,477
programs.

8
00:00:27,927 --> 00:00:32,023
There are libraries for math, graphics,
sound, maps, and much more.

9
00:00:33,173 --> 00:00:39,544
An API, an application program interface,
is the documentation that explains how to

10
00:00:39,544 --> 00:00:40,618
use a library.

11
00:00:41,068 --> 00:00:45,567
It lists each procedure's name, its
parameters, what it does, and what it

12
00:00:45,567 --> 00:00:46,060
returns.

13
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:53,893
For example, an API might say: drawCircle,
x comma y comma radius, draws a circle

14
00:00:53,893 --> 00:00:55,296
centered at x, y.

15
00:00:55,746 --> 00:00:58,626
You can use it correctly just from that
description.

16
00:00:59,776 --> 00:01:05,102
Libraries save time, and because they're
widely used and tested, they're often more

17
00:01:05,102 --> 00:01:07,925
reliable than code you'd write from
scratch.

18
00:01:08,375 --> 00:01:11,319
This is procedural abstraction on a large
scale.

19
00:01:12,469 --> 00:01:17,541
On the exam, read the description of a
procedure carefully: what each parameter

20
00:01:17,541 --> 00:01:19,274
means, and what comes back.

21
00:01:20,424 --> 00:01:25,842
Your turn. A library has a procedure,
round, x, that returns x rounded to the

22
00:01:25,842 --> 00:01:30,344
nearest whole number. What does round of
seven point six return?

23
00:01:30,794 --> 00:01:32,074
Pause the video now.

24
00:01:32,524 --> 00:01:36,542
Eight. You know that from the
documentation alone, without seeing the

25
00:01:36,542 --> 00:01:36,834
code.

26
00:01:37,284 --> 00:01:39,502
Next, we'll generate random values.

27
00:01:40,653 --> 00:01:44,834
Games, simulations and security all need
unpredictability.

28
00:01:45,284 --> 00:01:48,250
So programs need a way to make random
choices.

29
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:55,645
In AP pseudocode, RANDOM, a comma b,
returns a random whole number from a to b,

30
00:01:55,645 --> 00:01:57,464
including both a and b.

31
00:01:57,914 --> 00:02:00,730
Every value in that range is equally
likely.

32
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:08,021
RANDOM one comma six acts like rolling a
die. It can return one, two, three, four,

33
00:02:08,021 --> 00:02:08,920
five or six.

34
00:02:09,370 --> 00:02:12,591
Each time the program runs, the result may
be different.

35
00:02:13,741 --> 00:02:16,408
You can use random values to make choices.

36
00:02:16,858 --> 00:02:22,689
IF RANDOM one comma four equals one, the
event happens. That's a one in four, or

37
00:02:22,689 --> 00:02:24,730
twenty five percent, chance.

38
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:31,634
Because results change from run to run,
exam questions often ask which outputs are

39
00:02:31,634 --> 00:02:34,370
possible, not which output will happen.

40
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:40,823
To test random code, run it many times,
and check that every result is in the

41
00:02:40,823 --> 00:02:41,856
expected range.

42
00:02:43,006 --> 00:02:49,239
Your turn. x gets RANDOM one comma three,
plus RANDOM one comma three. What are the

43
00:02:49,239 --> 00:02:52,393
smallest and largest possible values of x?

44
00:02:52,843 --> 00:02:54,123
Pause the video now.

45
00:02:54,573 --> 00:02:59,864
Smallest: one plus one, which is two.
Largest: three plus three, which is six.

46
00:03:00,314 --> 00:03:03,258
Next, we'll use randomness to build
simulations.

47
00:03:04,407 --> 00:03:09,652
How do scientists test a new traffic light
pattern, or the spread of a disease,

48
00:03:09,652 --> 00:03:11,511
without risking real people?

49
00:03:11,961 --> 00:03:13,390
They use simulations.

50
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:19,063
A simulation is a program that imitates
something from the real world.

51
00:03:19,513 --> 00:03:23,289
It uses a model: a simplified version of
the real thing.

52
00:03:24,439 --> 00:03:29,367
To keep things manageable, simulations
leave out details that don't matter much.

53
00:03:29,817 --> 00:03:34,809
That makes them faster to build and run,
but it can also introduce bias if

54
00:03:34,809 --> 00:03:36,900
important details are left out.

55
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:43,544
Many simulations use random values to
model uncertainty, like whether a car

56
00:03:43,544 --> 00:03:45,815
arrives, or a coin lands heads.

57
00:03:46,265 --> 00:03:50,169
Running the simulation many times shows
what usually happens.

58
00:03:51,319 --> 00:03:56,659
Simulations are useful when real
experiments would be too expensive, too

59
00:03:56,659 --> 00:03:59,255
dangerous, too slow, or impossible.

60
00:03:59,705 --> 00:04:03,822
You can test many ideas quickly, and
change conditions easily.

61
00:04:04,972 --> 00:04:09,866
But a simulation is only as good as its
model. Its results should be checked

62
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:11,927
against real data when possible.

63
00:04:13,077 --> 00:04:18,445
Your turn. A simulation of a city's
traffic ignores bicycles. What's one risk

64
00:04:18,445 --> 00:04:19,840
of leaving them out?

65
00:04:20,290 --> 00:04:21,570
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:27,155
The results may be wrong or biased for
areas with many bicycles, so decisions

67
00:04:27,155 --> 00:04:28,889
based on it could be poor.

68
00:04:29,339 --> 00:04:32,155
Next, we'll measure how efficient an
algorithm is.

69
00:04:33,305 --> 00:04:38,577
Two algorithms can solve the same problem,
but one might take seconds while the other

70
00:04:38,577 --> 00:04:39,321
takes years.

71
00:04:39,771 --> 00:04:40,923
That's efficiency.

72
00:04:42,073 --> 00:04:46,745
Efficiency is about how the number of
steps grows as the input gets bigger.

73
00:04:47,195 --> 00:04:51,099
We don't time it on one computer. We count
how the work grows.

74
00:04:52,249 --> 00:04:55,961
Linear search grows like n: double the
list, double the work.

75
00:04:56,411 --> 00:05:02,278
Binary search grows like log n. Checking
all pairs in a list grows like n squared.

76
00:05:03,428 --> 00:05:08,977
Algorithms whose steps grow like a
polynomial, such as n, n squared, or n

77
00:05:08,977 --> 00:05:11,257
cubed, run in reasonable time.

78
00:05:11,707 --> 00:05:17,812
Algorithms that grow exponentially, like
two to the n, or like n factorial, run in

79
00:05:17,812 --> 00:05:19,152
unreasonable time.

80
00:05:20,302 --> 00:05:25,645
With two to the n, adding just one item
doubles the work. At one hundred items, it

81
00:05:25,645 --> 00:05:28,708
would take longer than the age of the
universe.

82
00:05:29,158 --> 00:05:33,147
Trying every possible route between many
cities is an example.

83
00:05:34,297 --> 00:05:40,237
On the exam, reasonable time means
polynomial or slower growth. Unreasonable

84
00:05:40,237 --> 00:05:43,598
time means exponential or factorial
growth.

85
00:05:44,748 --> 00:05:50,542
Your turn. An algorithm checks every
possible subset of a list of n items. Is

86
00:05:50,542 --> 00:05:53,175
it reasonable or unreasonable time?

87
00:05:53,625 --> 00:05:54,905
Pause the video now.

88
00:05:55,355 --> 00:06:00,048
There are two to the n subsets, so it's
exponential: unreasonable time.

89
00:06:00,498 --> 00:06:05,042
Next: heuristics, undecidable problems,
and the exam reference sheet.

90
00:06:06,193 --> 00:06:11,270
Some problems would take an unreasonable
amount of time to solve perfectly.

91
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:14,387
So we settle for a good answer, found
quickly.

92
00:06:15,537 --> 00:06:20,721
A heuristic is an approach that finds a
solution that's good enough, when finding

93
00:06:20,721 --> 00:06:22,833
the best solution is impractical.

94
00:06:23,283 --> 00:06:25,352
It trades a perfect answer for speed.

95
00:06:26,502 --> 00:06:32,220
Think of a delivery driver visiting twenty
cities. Checking every possible route is

96
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:33,116
unreasonable.

97
00:06:33,566 --> 00:06:37,960
A heuristic: always drive to the nearest
city you haven't visited yet.

98
00:06:39,110 --> 00:06:43,377
That nearest neighbor rule runs fast, and
usually gives a short route.

99
00:06:43,827 --> 00:06:48,136
It isn't guaranteed to be the shortest,
but it's close enough to be useful.

100
00:06:49,286 --> 00:06:53,468
Heuristics are everywhere: in map apps,
game AI, and scheduling.

101
00:06:53,918 --> 00:06:59,170
On the exam, choose a heuristic when an
exact solution would take unreasonable

102
00:06:59,170 --> 00:06:59,507
time.

103
00:07:00,657 --> 00:07:04,604
Your turn. Is a heuristic guaranteed to
find the best solution?

104
00:07:05,054 --> 00:07:06,334
Pause the video now.

105
00:07:06,784 --> 00:07:12,043
No. It finds a good enough solution in
reasonable time, but not always the best

106
00:07:12,043 --> 00:07:12,309
one.

107
00:07:12,759 --> 00:07:15,703
Next, problems no algorithm can ever
solve.

108
00:07:16,853 --> 00:07:20,928
Can every problem be solved by a computer,
if we're patient enough?

109
00:07:21,378 --> 00:07:22,636
Surprisingly, no.

110
00:07:23,786 --> 00:07:28,501
A decision problem has a yes or no answer,
like: is this number even?

111
00:07:28,951 --> 00:07:34,189
A decidable problem has an algorithm that
gives the correct answer for every

112
00:07:34,189 --> 00:07:35,223
possible input.

113
00:07:36,373 --> 00:07:41,985
An undecidable problem has no algorithm
that can always give a correct yes or no

114
00:07:41,985 --> 00:07:43,669
answer, for every input.

115
00:07:44,119 --> 00:07:47,212
Not a slow algorithm. No algorithm at all.

116
00:07:48,362 --> 00:07:54,157
The famous example is the halting problem:
given any program and its input, will the

117
00:07:54,157 --> 00:07:56,917
program eventually stop, or run forever?

118
00:07:57,367 --> 00:08:03,646
Alan Turing proved in 1936 that no
algorithm can answer this correctly for

119
00:08:03,646 --> 00:08:04,834
every program.

120
00:08:05,984 --> 00:08:09,994
An undecidable problem may still be
solvable for some inputs.

121
00:08:10,444 --> 00:08:14,711
It's just impossible to write one
algorithm that works for all of them.

122
00:08:15,861 --> 00:08:20,234
Your turn. Is is this number even?
decidable or undecidable?

123
00:08:20,684 --> 00:08:21,964
Pause the video now.

124
00:08:22,414 --> 00:08:28,409
Decidable. n MOD two equals zero answers
it correctly for every whole number.

125
00:08:28,859 --> 00:08:31,504
Next, let's tour the AP exam reference
sheet.

126
00:08:32,654 --> 00:08:37,262
On the AP exam, you get a reference sheet
with all the pseudocode you need.

127
00:08:37,712 --> 00:08:40,699
Knowing it well saves time and prevents
mistakes.

128
00:08:41,849 --> 00:08:46,382
Assignment and display: a arrow
expression, DISPLAY of expression, and

129
00:08:46,382 --> 00:08:49,038
INPUT, which returns what the user types.

130
00:08:50,188 --> 00:08:55,591
Math and logic: plus, minus, times,
divide, MOD, and the comparisons, plus

131
00:08:55,591 --> 00:08:56,759
NOT, AND and OR.

132
00:08:57,209 --> 00:09:02,243
RANDOM a comma b returns a random whole
number from a to b, inclusive.

133
00:09:03,393 --> 00:09:08,727
Control: IF and ELSE, REPEAT n TIMES,
REPEAT UNTIL, and FOR EACH item IN list.

134
00:09:09,177 --> 00:09:12,739
Procedures: PROCEDURE name with
parameters, and RETURN.

135
00:09:13,889 --> 00:09:19,376
Lists: square brackets, list bracket i,
INSERT, APPEND, REMOVE and LENGTH. And

136
00:09:19,376 --> 00:09:21,697
remember: the first index is one.

137
00:09:22,147 --> 00:09:27,869
The sheet also shows robot commands: MOVE
FORWARD, ROTATE LEFT, ROTATE RIGHT, and

138
00:09:27,869 --> 00:09:28,505
CAN MOVE.

139
00:09:29,655 --> 00:09:35,113
The same commands appear in both text and
block form. Practice reading both, so

140
00:09:35,113 --> 00:09:37,463
nothing surprises you on test day.

141
00:09:38,613 --> 00:09:44,793
Your turn. A robot faces right. It runs
ROTATE LEFT twice. Which way does it face

142
00:09:44,793 --> 00:09:45,098
now?

143
00:09:45,548 --> 00:09:46,828
Pause the video now.

144
00:09:47,278 --> 00:09:52,767
Each ROTATE LEFT turns ninety degrees
counterclockwise. Right, then up, then

145
00:09:52,767 --> 00:09:54,211
left. It faces left.

146
00:09:54,661 --> 00:10:00,128
That's the end of Big Idea three. Next,
Big Idea four: computer systems and

147
00:10:00,128 --> 00:10:00,784
networks.

148
00:10:01,934 --> 00:10:06,611
If this helped, subscribe to School
Computer Science, Explained, so you don't

149
00:10:06,611 --> 00:10:07,886
miss the next lesson.

150
00:10:08,336 --> 00:10:09,232
See you there.

